# ============================================================================
# tests/T22.bd  -  T22, file 2 of 2.  THE VICTIM.
# Run this one in the FOREGROUND, immediately after backgrounding
# T22_bomb. Also run it ALONE first, as ARM 0.
# ============================================================================
#
# PURPOSE: be a perfectly-paced, provably-harmless second process that does
# nothing but say "I am still alive, and here is what the CPU meter looks
# like from in here" once per second - and then see whether somebody else's
# overload takes it down, and whether it saw that coming.
#
# Its loop is the shape test_cpu_decay.bd already proved sits at a rock-
# steady cpu=10.0 indefinitely and never dies on its own (sleep(1) + a
# handful of lines), plus one save() line. Nothing in here can overload
# anything. ARM 0 proves that empirically rather than assuming it.
#
# TWO INSTRUMENTS, DELIBERATELY REDUNDANT:
#   1. log() to the terminal, once per poll. Foreground, so it prints live,
#      line by line, ~1.5s apart - each line is already on screen long
#      before the next one is due. The LAST line on screen is the readout.
#   2. save() to /home/Nykita/t22_victim.txt, overwritten every poll so the
#      file always holds the LATEST beat. Terminal-flush semantics at the
#      instant of death have never been verified in this project, and the
#      one line that matters most is the last one - this file is the backup
#      copy of exactly that line. It is also the ONLY readout in ARM 2,
#      where this script runs backgrounded and its log() is redirected.
#   Both carry the SAME cpu value: get_cpu() is called once per poll and
#   hoisted into c, so the terminal and the file can never disagree.
#
# WHAT EACH FIELD IS FOR
#   n    poll counter. Monotone - the one number that proves liveness.
#        Cat the file twice 10s apart: n frozen = dead, n advancing = alive
#        but silent. That distinction is the whole point of the double-cat.
#   t    seconds since THIS script started - readability only.
#   u    ABSOLUTE unix time. This is what aligns against the bomb file's
#        burn_start. Any poll with u >= burn_start happened while the killer
#        was firing; those cpu values are the secondary result.
#   cpu  get_cpu(), sampled right after a sleep(1) - i.e. at exactly the
#        point in the cycle where menu.bd's throttle()/cpu_guard_*() guards
#        sample it. This is the number the whole investigation has been
#        trusting.
#
# WHAT EACH POSSIBLE RESULT MEANS
#   No "T22 VICTIM DONE" line, last u ~= burn_start + 5, file frozen on both
#   cats, ps lists nothing
#       -> SESSION-WIDE KILL. The banner tells the truth: one process's
#          overload really does terminate every other running script.
#   All 40 polls print, "T22 VICTIM DONE" appears, and the bomb file is
#   frozen at state=BURNING
#       -> PER-PROCESS KILL. Only the offender dies. "every running script
#          gets killed" is wrong, and every "no other process was running"
#          assurance in this investigation stops being reassuring and starts
#          being irrelevant. Run ARM 2 before concluding.
#   Then, on the session-wide branch ONLY, look at every cpu value with
#   u >= burn_start:
#       all <= C0 + 10 (i.e. still at the ~10.0 idle floor)
#         -> THE METER IS BLIND TO THE KILLER. get_cpu() reads "safe" at the
#            exact sample point our guards use, right up to the instant every
#            process dies. This single result would explain why widening
#            cpu_guard thresholds changed nothing, why throttle() cost a flat
#            ~3.7s regardless of preceding work, and why a logged cpu=100 sat
#            harmlessly for 840s in test_decode_pacing.bd. It means no guard
#            built on get_cpu() can ever be made to work.
#       any >= 60
#         -> THE METER SEES THE KILLER. get_cpu() does aggregate across
#            processes fast enough to be a usable guard, and the guard design
#            in menu.bd is sound in principle.
#       in between
#         -> ambiguous. Paste every value verbatim, do not round or summarise.
#
# HOW TO RUN
#   ARM 0 (do this FIRST, once - it is the gate):
#     ps                          (confirm nothing is running)
#     run T22
#     -> must print "T22 VICTIM DONE - 40 polls, never killed".
#        Record the steady cpu value it holds; call it C0 (expect ~10.0).
#        If it dies, or the trace is not flat and low, this instrument is
#        invalid - cut the body down to test_cpu_decay.bd's exact 3-line
#        idle shape (drop the save() line and the hoisted c) and redo ARM 0.
#   ARM 1 (3 runs; stop at 2 if both agree on both readouts):
#     ps
#     run T22_bomb &
#     run T22         (IMMEDIATELY - within ~5s)
#     ...watch, touch nothing. Burn starts 15s after the bomb launched,
#        which is ~10-14s into this script's timeline: expect poll 8-12...
#     ps                          (the moment it is over)
#     cat /home/Nykita/t22_bomb.txt
#     cat /home/Nykita/t22_victim.txt
#     ...wait 10s...
#     cat /home/Nykita/t22_victim.txt      (did n advance?)
#     kill <pid> anything still listed, before the next run.
#   ARM 2 (ONLY if ARM 1 said per-process, once):
#     run T22 &
#     run T22_bomb 5
#     then cat the victim file twice, 10s apart.
#
# PASTE BACK: the complete terminal transcript including the overload banner
# verbatim, both ps outputs, the bomb cat, and BOTH victim cats. Say
# explicitly which victim line was the last one printed.
#
# PATHS: /home/Nykita/ is assumed to be home - must match T22_bomb.bd.
# @param polls "how many 1-second polls before a clean exit" default=40

var POLLS = int(get_param("polls", "40"))
var F = "/home/Nykita/t22_victim.txt"
var t0 = get_unix_time()
var c = get_cpu()
save(F, "n=0 u=" + str(t0) + " cpu=" + str(c) + " state=START")
sleep(1)
log("T22 VICTIM START u=" + str(t0) + " cpu=" + str(c) + " polls=" + str(POLLS))
var n = 0
while n < POLLS:
    sleep(1)
    n = n + 1
    c = get_cpu()
    log("T22 v n=" + str(n) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s u=" + str(get_unix_time()) + " cpu=" + str(c))
    save(F, "n=" + str(n) + " u=" + str(get_unix_time()) + " cpu=" + str(c) + " state=POLL")
save(F, "n=" + str(n) + " u=" + str(get_unix_time()) + " cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " state=DONE")
log("T22 VICTIM DONE - " + str(n) + " polls, never killed, cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
